--- body ---
## Evidence

On 2026-09-17, while propagating the new bootstrap binary across nine local
installations, both the human operator and the executing agent understood
`erg update` to mean "obtain the current binary from the git-erg project".
The command instead fetched `tickets/erg` from each adopter project's own
`origin`; the rollout therefore required an explicit `ERG_UPDATE_URL` override.

This is not hypothetical friction.  `climate-finance-het` had previously
documented that the same interpretation left its binary on an old revision
from June until September: a current checkout asks its own current origin for
the binary it already has, then reports `erg: already up to date` without ever
consulting git-erg.

The implementation and long help mention `origin`, but the user-facing model
is still misleading:

- README says only "the git remote";
- the integration guide prescribes `erg update && erg init` without explaining
  how a new git-erg binary first enters an adopter repository;
- `already up to date` does not name the source or say that git-erg was not
  checked;
- the verb `update` conventionally suggests upstream currency, while the
  actual default operation is clone-to-project synchronization.

## Decision

Name the operation for what it does: `erg sync`.

- `erg sync` synchronizes `<ticket store>/erg` with the binary committed
  at the adopter project's `origin` default branch.  This remains the default
  and preserves project-level review and pinning.  It never replaces a system
  or PATH binary used to launch the command.
- `erg sync --upstream` explicitly fetches the binary from the git-erg project.
  The upstream source must be named unambiguously in help and output; this mode
  is opt-in because it bypasses the adopter repository's pinned binary.
- `erg update` remains a compatibility alias during the transition and points
  users to `erg sync`; it must not silently acquire a different meaning.

The implementation continues to shell out to git.  Do not add an HTTP client
or any other network dependency.  Preserve the atomic write-temp-then-rename
swap, ticket-store anchoring, offline no-op behavior, legacy-header hint, and
post-swap asset diagnostics.

The existing `ERG_UPDATE_URL` and `.ergrc [update] url` overrides need an
explicit compatibility decision and documented precedence relative to
`--upstream`.  Whatever precedence is chosen, the command must print the
resolved source so that no successful or no-op result can be mistaken for a
check of another source.

## Required interface

- Short help describes `sync` as synchronizing the project's vendored binary
  from its origin, not as obtaining the latest git-erg release.
- Success and no-op messages name the resolved source and distinguish project
  origin from git-erg upstream.
- `fetch` is not used as the command name: unlike `git fetch`, this operation
  mutates the working tree by replacing the executable.
- Documentation separates two workflows:
  1. clone synchronization: `erg sync`, then init with the newly synchronized
     traveler on Linux x86-64 or an equivalently updated native system binary;
  2. explicit upstream import: `erg sync --upstream`, review or verify
     `tickets/erg` before first execution, then init with the platform-appropriate
     updated binary, review the asset diff, and commit the binary and assets.

## Exit criteria

- [x] `erg sync` fetches the adopter project's `origin` and atomically installs
      its committed `tickets/erg`.
- [x] `erg sync --upstream` fetches the git-erg project explicitly and
      atomically installs its committed `tickets/erg`.
- [x] `erg update` remains a tested compatibility alias whose output directs
      users to `erg sync`.
- [x] Every updated/already-current message identifies the resolved source and
      cannot imply that git-erg upstream was checked when it was not.
- [x] Tests give project origin and git-erg upstream different binary bytes and
      prove that the default and `--upstream` select opposite expected sources;
      each arm first proves its hashes differ so a vacuous fixture cannot pass.
- [x] Tests pin precedence and compatibility for `ERG_UPDATE_URL` and
      `.ergrc [update] url`.
- [x] Atomic replacement, offline behavior, store anchoring, migration hints,
      and post-swap asset reporting retain coverage.
- [x] README, generated manual, `erg integration`, command summaries, and all
      canonical `update && init` guidance use and explain the new interface.
- [x] `make test`, `make validate`, and `make ready` pass.
